    {
      "title": "The pinning-field line defect in thermal geometries",
      "speaker": "Julien Barrat",
      "affiliation": "Princeton University",
      "time": "2:00 PM",
      "location": "SCGP Common Room",
      "description": "In recent years, conformal bootstrap methods have been extended to settings in which conformal symmetry is broken in a controlled manner. Conformal line defects provide a prominent example, with applications in both condensed-matter physics and holography. Such defects induce a one-dimensional CFT along the line, making them particularly well suited to bootstrap methods. In parallel, thermal bootstrap methods have yielded new results for CFTs at finite temperature in both infinite and finite volume.\n\nIn this talk, I will present ongoing work combining defect and finite-temperature bootstrap methods to study two-point functions of operators supported on a conformal line defect wrapping the thermal circle. I will discuss general constraints on these correlators and show that the finite-volume defect problem simplifies drastically compared with its bulk counterpart. Analyticity and thermal periodicity motivate a periodized formulation of the defect OPE, leading to an analytic bootstrap problem.\n\nThe framework is then applied to the conformal pinning-field line defect in critical O(N) models. At one loop in the epsilon-expansion, it determines the thermal correlators of the lowest-lying defect scalars up to a finite (and small) number of constants. I will also present a Padé estimate in d=3 for the thermal one-point function of the lowest-lying scalar, providing a concrete benchmark for future thermal bootstrap studies. If time permits, I will discuss the large-N setup and conclude by outlining related future directions.",
      "date": "2026-09-10",
      "series": "Journal Club",
      "link": ""
    },
    {
      "title": "The 5th C.N. Yang Colloquium by Dr. Sheldon Glashow (Nobel Laureate in Physics 1979)",
      "speaker": "",
      "affiliation": "",
      "time": "3:15 PM",
      "location": "Simons Center for Geometry and Physics, State University of New York, Stony Brook, NY 11794, USA",
      "description": "The 5th C.N. Yang Colloquium will be held on September 15, 2026.\nThe colloquium will be given by Dr. Sheldon Glashow who shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Physics for his contribution to the theory of the unified weak and electromagnetic interaction.  The title of his talk is, \"My Memories of Frank Yang\".  \n\nThis colloquium is even more special as we commemorate Prof. CN \"Frank\" Yang who passed away last year and Yang–Mills theory provided the gauge-theory framework for the Standard Model, whose development began with Dr. Glashow’s groundbreaking 1961 paper on electroweak unification.\n\nCoffee/Tea: 3:15pm, Simons Center Lobby\nLecture: 3:45pm, Della Pietra Family Auditorium, SCGP Room 103.",
      "date": "2026-09-15",
      "series": "YITP Calendar",
      "link": ""
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimizing ice cubes",
      "speaker": "Israel Klich",
      "affiliation": "University  of Virginia",
      "time": "2:00 PM",
      "location": "313",
      "description": "The Mpemba effect—the counterintuitive possibility that a hotter system can cool faster than a colder one—is part of a broader class of anomalous relaxation phenomena in which distance from equilibrium does not determine relaxation time. I will describe how such effects arise from the spectral structure of stochastic dynamics: different initial states excite the slow relaxation modes with different amplitudes, and an initially distant state may relax rapidly if its overlap with the dominant slow mode is small or vanishes. I will then introduce the idea of an “optimal ice cube”: a suitably prepared auxiliary system which, when briefly coupled to the system of interest, suppresses its slowest relaxation mode and thereby accelerates equilibration. This provides a simple framework for designing optimal thermalization protocols and illustrates how nonequilibrium resources can be used to control relaxation.",
      "date": "2026-09-16",
      "series": "Wednesday Seminar",
      "link": ""
    },
    {
      "title": "Bootstrapping Pion Form Factors at Large N",
      "speaker": "DIlara Kosva",
      "affiliation": "YITP",
      "time": "2:00 PM",
      "location": "SCGP Common Room",
      "description": "We will start by reviewing the program of carving out the space of large N theories by bootstrapping the scattering of pions. Using analyticity, unitarity and Regge boundedness of the 2->2 pion amplitude, one can derive two-sided bounds on the EFT couplings and identify the allowed regions in the space of theories. One limitation of this system is that the bootstrap is oblivious to the short-distance dynamics of QCD, where perturbation theory applies. A canonical way to include this UV information is to probe the theory with local gauge-invariant operators, whose high-energy behavior is controlled by asymptotic freedom. We will consider a mixed system of the vector-current two-point function, the pion vector form factor, and the pion scattering amplitude. Rigorous bounds can be derived on low-energy coefficients of the form factor, and by inputting perturbative results through the two-point function, one can also obtain bounds on various couplings that are more phenomenological in nature. We will see at the end how the UV input shrinks the space of allowed theories towards where Large N QCD is expected to sit. Based on arXiv:2606.19420.",
      "date": "2026-09-17",
      "series": "Journal Club",
      "link": ""
    },
